WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:08.561
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.861 --> 00:00:17.097
Unit fifty-seven: Communication Across Cultures. In this unit, you will understand cultural differences in communication.

4
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:19.769
high-context culture.

5
00:00:20.469 --> 00:00:26.788
high-context culture means a culture where meaning depends on context, tone and relationships.

6
00:00:27.088 --> 00:00:30.016
Cambodia is generally a high-context culture.

7
00:00:30.716 --> 00:00:34.692
Your turn. Say: high-context culture.

8
00:00:37.392 --> 00:00:39.125
high-context culture.

9
00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:41.768
low-context culture.

10
00:00:42.468 --> 00:00:48.438
low-context culture means a culture where meaning is stated directly in words.

11
00:00:48.738 --> 00:00:53.234
The USA is often described as a low-context culture.

12
00:00:53.934 --> 00:00:57.561
Your turn. Say: low-context culture.

13
00:01:00.261 --> 00:01:02.284
low-context culture.

14
00:01:03.283 --> 00:01:05.236
face-saving.

15
00:01:05.936 --> 00:01:10.328
face-saving means avoiding embarrassment for yourself or others.

16
00:01:10.628 --> 00:01:14.624
A face-saving solution allowed both sides to agree.

17
00:01:15.324 --> 00:01:19.045
Your turn. Say: face-saving.

18
00:01:21.745 --> 00:01:22.827
face-saving.

19
00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:25.538
indirect communication.

20
00:01:26.238 --> 00:01:32.301
indirect communication means expressing meaning through hints rather than direct statements.

21
00:01:32.601 --> 00:01:36.411
'Maybe later' is often indirect communication for 'no'.

22
00:01:37.111 --> 00:01:40.913
Your turn. Say: indirect communication.

23
00:01:43.613 --> 00:01:45.299
indirect communication.

24
00:01:46.283 --> 00:01:47.737
power distance.

25
00:01:48.437 --> 00:01:53.525
power distance means how much a culture accepts unequal power between people.

26
00:01:53.825 --> 00:01:57.833
In high power distance cultures, people rarely question their boss.

27
00:01:58.533 --> 00:02:01.534
Your turn. Say: power distance.

28
00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:05.513
power distance.

29
00:02:06.517 --> 00:02:07.762
individualism.

30
00:02:08.462 --> 00:02:13.654
individualism means valuing personal goals and independence.

31
00:02:13.954 --> 00:02:17.405
Individualism is strong in many Western societies.

32
00:02:18.105 --> 00:02:21.279
Your turn. Say: individualism.

33
00:02:23.979 --> 00:02:25.340
individualism.

34
00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:27.572
collectivism.

35
00:02:28.272 --> 00:02:31.653
collectivism means valuing group goals and harmony.

36
00:02:31.953 --> 00:02:35.369
Collectivism shapes family decisions in Cambodia.

37
00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:39.394
Your turn. Say: collectivism.

38
00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:43.212
collectivism.

39
00:02:44.217 --> 00:02:45.938
intercultural competence.

40
00:02:46.638 --> 00:02:51.737
intercultural competence means the ability to communicate effectively across cultures.

41
00:02:52.037 --> 00:02:55.848
Intercultural competence is essential for global careers.

42
00:02:56.548 --> 00:03:00.187
Your turn. Say: intercultural competence.

43
00:03:02.887 --> 00:03:04.527
intercultural competence.

44
00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:07.644
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:07.944 --> 00:03:12.800
Rithy, when I first came to Cambodia, I made a lot of cultural mistakes.

46
00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:15.865
Really? Like what?

47
00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:21.788
In meetings, I asked very direct questions. Nobody would answer openly.

48
00:03:23.083 --> 00:03:28.116
That's typical of a high-context culture. People want to avoid losing face.

49
00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:33.100
I had to pay close attention to tone and body language.

50
00:03:34.383 --> 00:03:39.091
And power distance plays a big role. Juniors rarely contradict seniors.

51
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:43.393
Now I make a point of asking for opinions privately.

52
00:03:44.683 --> 00:03:49.576
That's real intercultural competence. You've learned to read between the lines.

53
00:03:50.883 --> 00:03:54.172
Grammar focus: Collocations (mastery).

54
00:03:54.472 --> 00:03:58.131
Collocations make your English sound natural and precise.

55
00:03:58.431 --> 00:04:03.995
Pay close attention to tone, avoid losing face, and read between the lines.

56
00:04:04.295 --> 00:04:07.282
Power distance plays a big role in communication.

57
00:04:07.582 --> 00:04:13.575
Collocations about communication are especially useful in international workplaces.

58
00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:18.350
Now read and listen. Lost in Cultural Translation.

59
00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:22.542
Speaking the same language does not guarantee mutual understanding.

60
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:29.007
Anthropologist Edward Hall distinguished between high-context and low-context cultures.

61
00:04:30.317 --> 00:04:37.030
In high-context cultures, much of the meaning lies in tone, relationships and what is left unsaid.

62
00:04:38.317 --> 00:04:42.766
In low-context cultures, people tend to say exactly what they mean.

63
00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:50.543
Concepts such as face-saving and power distance shape how disagreement is expressed.

64
00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:58.029
Collectivism encourages harmony, while individualism values direct personal opinions.

65
00:04:59.317 --> 00:05:05.798
Misunderstandings often arise when people interpret indirect communication as dishonesty.

66
00:05:07.083 --> 00:05:12.020
Intercultural competence means learning to read between the lines without judging.

67
00:05:13.317 --> 00:05:16.271
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:16.571 --> 00:05:20.103
A culture where meaning is stated directly is a blank.

69
00:05:20.403 --> 00:05:26.344
A, high-context culture. B, low-context culture. C, collectivism.

70
00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:33.209
The answer is B: low-context culture.

71
00:05:34.717 --> 00:05:37.529
Please pay blank attention to the instructions.

72
00:05:37.829 --> 00:05:41.180
A, close. B, near. C, tight.

73
00:05:45.183 --> 00:05:47.194
The answer is A: close.

74
00:05:48.683 --> 00:05:51.368
He didn't want to lose blank in front of his team.

75
00:05:51.668 --> 00:05:54.985
A, face. B, head. C, eye.

76
00:05:58.983 --> 00:06:01.250
The answer is A: face.

77
00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.794
Valuing group goals and harmony is blank.

78
00:06:06.094 --> 00:06:11.083
A, individualism. B, collectivism. C, power distance.

79
00:06:15.083 --> 00:06:17.582
The answer is B: collectivism.

80
00:06:19.083 --> 00:06:23.033
According to the text, what does intercultural competence mean?

81
00:06:23.333 --> 00:06:30.087
A, speaking fast. B, reading between the lines without judging. C, avoiding foreigners.

82
00:06:34.083 --> 00:06:37.685
The answer is B: reading between the lines without judging.

83
00:06:39.183 --> 00:06:44.773
Now it's your turn. Part one: What advice would you give a foreigner working in Cambodia?

84
00:06:50.073 --> 00:06:56.310
Part two: Use three collocations: give two pieces of advice and explain each.

85
00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:14.736
I'd advise them to pay close attention to tone and to avoid making anyone lose face in public. Disagreement should be expressed privately, because harmony plays a big role in Cambodian workplaces.

86
00:07:15.036 --> 00:07:17.442
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:23.436
Great work today! You learned eight new words and collocations (mastery).

88
00:07:23.736 --> 00:07:28.292
Today's value is respect for culture. Be proud of our Khmer culture.

89
00:07:28.592 --> 00:07:31.442
See you in Unit fifty-eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:31.742 --> 00:07:34.824
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

